<?nvs
// `[control] socket` is how an administrator talks to a running Novis server:
// one local socket on the machine the server is on, and nothing else. There is
// no port to open, no token to hand out and nothing to log into — who owns the
// socket is the authentication, and the server refuses to start if any other
// account on the machine could write the directory holding it.
//
// This checkout writes the one value that turns it off, because nothing here is
// a long-running server:
//
//     [control]
//     socket = false

echo 'control socket: ', Core\Config::get('control.socket') ?? '(none)', "\n";

// A server writes a path instead — `/run/nvs/control.sock`, or a pipe name such
// as `\\.\pipe\nvs-control` on Windows — and `nvs ctl reload` and `nvs ctl config`
// then reach it over that path. What can never be written here is a host and a
// port: an address another machine could dial is refused when the server starts,
// rather than being put behind a password.

// Where it lives is the operator's decision and a restart's work, never a
// running program's. The socket is created once, when the server starts, so a
// different name means a different server.
echo 'move it:        ', Core\Config::set('control.socket', '/tmp/mine.sock') ? 'yes' : 'no', "\n";
